Judy Barford example Lamesa hub is a  striking and gritty portrayal of police  violence social exclusion and childhood  the oscar-nominated French film is set  in the Paris suburb of morph ma it  reveals the hardship of people's lives  but steers clear of cliches as it  follows three low paid police officers  battling to keep the peace but with a  heavy-handed approach that makes things  worse  many of the film's actors a residents  from a housing estate non professionals  including activist al nami kanuti who  plays an enigmatic owner of a fast food  restaurant to whom police and young  people turn to for advice for decades  we've seen a denial of police violence  recently we saw the repression faced by  the yellow vests protesters where many  people discovered police violence but we  have always known about it because  today's repression was refined for years  in the suburbs portraying life in the  suburbs came naturally to director latch  Li who grew up in Marfa Mae and began  filming police violence as a pastime he  says that for decades French governments  have ignored the suburbs where  unemployment high and Opportunity scarce  he hopes the film will act as a wake-up  call nothing's changing schools in the  suburbs are a catastrophe cultural  associations had their subsidies cut so  this film is a cry for help to say  listen to us because it's more than 20  years that it's been going badly and we  feel like no one hears us lemmie Sahab  is bleak but its success has delighted  residents in more thermae at the local  football club few imagined that a story  set in their neighborhood would be  nominated for an Oscar the fact that the  director largely comes from more film a  is obviously a huge source of pride here  and it is particularly exciting for  these teenagers because the filmmakers  and the Football Club are flying some of  them to Los Angeles for the occasion  Oscar we started despite the reality  there are so many talented people in the  suburbs  sadly proves that even young people in  the suburbs can make it at the airport  that countdown to Hollywood begins the  trips a unique opportunity to explore  new horizons represent France and cheer  on largely a local hero who's inspired  them to believe in their dreams and  prove that it doesn't matter where you  come from it's where you're going that  counts  Natasha Butler al Jazeera Paris
 

